#### Technical Implementation:

1. We populate `contentExtensions[:muralId].smart-container` with the following data:
```json
{
    "data" : {
        "typeName" : "smart-container",
        "widgets" : {
            "0-1739915051824" : [
                {
                    "type": "apply-tag",
                    "payload": {
                        "tag": "tag1"
                    }
                },
                {
                    "type": "change-color",
                    "payload": {
                        "color": "red"
                    }
                },
                {
                    "type": "change-shape",
                    "payload": {
                        "color": "circle"
                    }
                }
            ]
        }
    },
    "entityId": ":muralId|:templateId"
}
```

2. We annotate the client-side widget model with this data as a string in the `containerRules` key:
```json
{
    "containerRules": "apply-tag:tag1\nchange-color:red\nchange-shape:circle"
}
```

**NOTE**: Some may consider it controversial to extend the widget model, but please note the
following.

i. This is not a complex data structure, it is just a string.
ii. This is a client-side only data-model change. We derive the value of this key from the content-extension record in the database. The necessity of doing this arises from the 
`widget-changes` system otherwise requiring access to the content-extentions `sdk`.

